WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.520
The Cal ISO has started rotating power outages.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.149
Governor Newsom today, address the recent rolling blackout.

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:07.200
We failed to predict and plan.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.480
Energy conservation is what the California
Independent System Operator wants to see.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.720
Any type of conservation...

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.240
The overall picture is that the California
grid ran out of energy.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.980
Critics say California's rolling blackouts this summer
were caused by decades of costly and poorly

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:30.250
planned decisions to replace coal, nuclear,
and gas power plants with solar and wind.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.830
Speaks to the delusion of California policy
makers.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:38.860
They really convinced themselves that they
could manage all of this increased demand

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.030
on renewables, which are fundamentally unreliable.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.610
Michael Shellenberger is the president of
Environmental Progress, which advocates for

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:54.040
greater reliance on nuclear power as a way
to reduce CO2 emissions and provide reliable energy.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:00.220
California banned the construction of new
nuclear reactors in 1976 and has been incentivizing

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:05.530
companies to close older plants by piling
on burdensome regulation ever since.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.499
Shellenberger says this loss has made California
more susceptible to blackouts.

00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:14.799
We were looking at about a 4,000 megawatt
shortfall, the two nuclear plants, the one

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.040
that shut down and the one they want to shut
down, total 4,400 megawatts.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.380
So it would have just provided the energy
that we didn't have.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.120
The other thing is that the nuclear plant,
unlike the solar farms or the wind, is reliable

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.300
92% of the year, the only times you can't
have it is when you're adding new fuel to the plant.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:39.280
Policymakers also started closing down natural
gas plants because they produce more CO2 emissions

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.240
than wind and solar, ignoring warnings that
doing so would lead to shortages.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.639
And Governor Gavin Newsom recently signed
an executive order asking the state legislature

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.180
to ban gas fracking for oil and gas.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:57.380
But the problem with wind and solar is that they
only provide consistent energy under

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.260
optimal weather conditions.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.040
And they just shut down too many of them.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.920
There's a lot of fancy dancing to explain
how it wasn't a result of renewables,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:12.400
but it was fundamentally that the sun goes down
at the exact moment that peak demand starts

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.420
to climb.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:20.400
But a major operator of California's grid says that it wasn't renewables like wind and solar

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.140
that caused the blackouts, but a lack of easily accessible back up energy.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.320
Renewables have not caused this issue.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.220
This is a resource issue, not a renewable
issue.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.600
Some defenders of renewable energy even say
that fossil fuels are the real culprit and

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:43.110
and that critics like Shellenberger are distorting
the facts in service of their preconceived biases.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:48.920
The August blackout, they point out, was directly
caused by the failure of a natural gas generator.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.540
Those fossil fuel technologies have trouble
performing in the heat.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.120
In fact, solar generation doesn't have trouble
performing in the heat.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:02.840
Energy analyst Amol Phadke is the coauthor
of UC Berkeley's 2035 report, which argues

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:09.079
that America should transition to 90% carbon
free energy generation in the next 15 years.

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:13.739
But the natural gas generator that failed
was a backup system, which was flipped on

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:19.500
only because the state's energy capacity failed
as Californians blasted their air conditioners

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.820
to deal with a heat wave as the sun went down
and the wind slowed.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.980
Those had to come online because they are
the backup for the renewables that-

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.160
Oh, those were kind of planned generation.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:31.660
Okay.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:37.240
The way you plan the power system is that,
you know the sun is going to set.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:42.080
Phadke says the failure to adequately plan
back up power was the real problem.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:51.310
And in fact, I would argue that having lot
more renewable energy and storage will make

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.280
the grid even more robust.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:00.099
As California has increased its reliance on
renewable energy, it has also become increasingly

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:03.640
reliant on energy imported from neighboring
states.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.409
They're counting on their neighbors too much,
and those neighbors need their power plants

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:09.409
cause they're hot too.

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:14.650
Engineer and investor Mark Mills is a faculty
fellow at Northwestern University and a senior

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.080
fellow at the Manhattan Institute.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.840
He says that California's push to replace
traditional power plants with renewables has

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.710
created a shortage of what's known as dispatchable
capacity, or generators that can be flipped

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.740
on when there's a spike in demand.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.440
What would happen in a normal grid is another
gas turbine would fire up and there'd be no blackout.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.640
What happened in the first blackouts is that
they didn't have dispatchable capacity,

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:42.840
and worse than that, there was a wind lull, so
simultaneous to the gas turbine not coming online,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.020
then you have to force blackouts to keep
the rest of the grid lit.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.280
None of that would have happened if you were
using conventional capacity.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.200
And the more wind you add, the less dispatchable
you have, the more likely you'll have those

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.890
events occur cause you'll have fewer and fewer
dispatchable assets.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.650
It's simple logic.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:03.920
Phadke says the solution is for California to build even more solar power plants,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.200
and invest more money in giant batteries that can store power from the wind and sun during off hours.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:16.500
In the long run, if you have enough batteries
to transfer that solar energy during the day

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.320
into the evening hours, you are good.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.110
And the good news is that the cost of those
batteries has dropped by 90% since 2010.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.640
The constant babbling about batteries is an
embarrassing failure to understand arithmetic.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.000
Mills has calculated that storing a barrel
of oil's worth of energy in a battery

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.310
costs at least a hundred times as much.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.870
And that it would take about a thousand years
for the world's largest battery factory

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.780
to produce enough to store just two days worth
of America's energy needs.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:49.000
Batteries are never going to get cheaper to
store energy than storing oil in a barrel

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.240
until we develop a room temperature superconductor,
which doesn't exist, could exist in theory,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.530
and could become cheap in theory, sometime.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.270
If that ever happens, we could store electricity
as cheap as oil in a barrel.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.300
Changes the world.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:07.680
Mills also points out that the intensive mining required to produce batteries has a major environmental cost.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.540
Wind and solar are very diffuse energy sources.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:16.120
So the amount of land required is order of
magnitude 10 to 30 times more than for hydrocarbons.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.260
Because they're diffuse energy sources, you
have to build lots of machines.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.760
And when you build lots of machines, you have
to mine more stuff.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.800
America doesn't like mines anymore so we depend
on mines as most people by now know, Cobalt

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:31.230
half of it's mined in Democratic Republic
of Congo, Chinese mine the rare earths,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.280
we don't anymore.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.280
The biggest nickel mines in the world are in the Arctic and Russia,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.160
Australia does a lot of the mining for the world.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.990
We can mine more, but the increase in mining
that the green energy path will require

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.410
will be the biggest increase in mineral extraction
the world has ever seen.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:49.970
Instead of producing our own oil and gas and
energy, which is 80% of our energy, we will

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.150
import energy minerals from somebody else
that will increase imports,

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:56.130
increase strategic dependencies, increase deficits.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.650
You may think that's fine, but it's a real
cost that no one's counting.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:00.890
It's dishonest.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:06.300
But the 2035 report estimates the cost of
not quickly pivoting to renewables

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:14.380
at $1.2 trillion in health and environmental damages and 85,000 premature deaths by 2050.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:19.520
It recommends a combination of emission standards,
government subsidies, and tax incentives

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.060
to ramp up solar, wind, and battery production as quickly as possible.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.220
Why not just say we're going to set a standard
and then however you get there,

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:36.120
figure that out, rather than tipping the scale towards solar and wind?

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.660
Yeah, I think that's the first best option.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:44.080
I would say that I think the simplest option,
but which is not politically acceptable,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.880
is tax the polluters.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:51.360
I mean, you have to pay your fair share of
what damage you do.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.860
But Shellenburger says that nuclear would provide
the clean and abundant energy that both sides want.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.040
If only California and other states
would stop creating incentives for them to

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:05.300
close down, and allow new ones to open up.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:09.590
Just keeping the nuclear plants online would
have kept prices down significantly.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:17.500
If California had a vision of being like France, 75% nuclear, getting our cooking and heating from electricity,

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:20.800
well that could be a very good deal for both consumers and the natural environment.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.320
But nobody's talking about that.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:28.000
And Mill says that ironically, the technological
innovations that would be required to fulfill

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.140
the environmentalists' dreams, rely on continuing
to have abundant energy right now.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:39.600
If you want to go from propellers to jet engines,
if you want to go from combustion to nuclear fission,

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.460
if you want those kinds of phenomenology
changes, if you want to store electricity

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:48.120
as cheap as we store oil, you need a different,
whole new solution.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:52.500
Because we always have new discoveries, but
they don't come from directed development

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.270
where a government says, "make that wind turbine
better, build me more wind turbines."

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:59.780
To get the kinds of shifts that people are
talking about in society, to get something

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:04.940
as profound as discovering of nuclear fission,
takes discoveries in basic science.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.890
So you produce energy at the least possible
cost to have as much profit to invest in basic

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:13.120
science and invest in adaptation and resilience.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.780
There's a respectable chance a lot of America
will follow in California's path.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.160
I think that's the path we're on.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.040
This is what their plan is.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:27.200
So, if you are concerned about the blackouts,
the six-fold increase in electricity prices,

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.770
the bad management of our electrical grid
that causes fires in places where we should

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:35.900
have less fires, not more then, yeah, you should be concerned about what's happening in California

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.580
and not want it to be imposed
on the rest of the U.S.

